	\uses{def:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line}
	Write $\widetilde D_{\mathrm v}$ for the seed-bearing refinement of
	$D_{\mathrm v}$ in Definition~\ref{def:line-point-dist}, where
	$\mathrm v\in\{\mathrm{ALine},\mathrm{DLine}\}$, and let
	$\widetilde D_{\mathrm v,i}$ be its normalized restriction to
	$\chi(s)=i$. Equivalently, condition the corresponding CL distribution
	on this seed event before decoding the line description. The event has
	probability $1/m$, so each restricted law has total mass one. An axis
	description still records $(u_0,s)$; distinct seeds in $\chi^{-1}(i)$
	remain distinct outcomes. A diagonal description records $(u_0,s,v)$,
	with $v_j=0$ for $j<i$. The maps
	\[
		((u_0,s),u)\longmapsto ((u_0,e_{\chi(s)}),u),\qquad
		((u_0,s,v),u)\longmapsto ((u_0,s,v),u)
	\]
	push these refined restrictions forward to the source restrictions
	$D_{\mathrm{ALine},i}$ and $D_{\mathrm{DLine},i}$, respectively.
	Only the axis map forgets the residual seed. The formalized definitions
	here are the refined restrictions, not their source push-forwards.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Mixtures and nonnegative averages on seed-bearing lines]
	\label{lem:restricted-line-refined-mixture-bounds}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.linePointDist_eq_mixture_restricted}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.avg_restricted_le, MIPStarRE.QPBT.avg_restricted_prod_le}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:ith-restricted-line-refined, def:line-point-dist}
	Each refined law is a uniform mixture of its restrictions:
	\[
		\widetilde D_{\mathrm v}
		=\frac1m\sum_{i=1}^m\widetilde D_{\mathrm v,i}.
	\]
	For admissible $(q,m,d)$, put
	$\widetilde D_{\mathrm{Line}}
	=\tfrac12(\widetilde D_{\mathrm{ALine}}+\widetilde D_{\mathrm{DLine}})$.
	If a nonnegative real-valued function has average at most $\delta$
	under $\widetilde D_{\mathrm{Line}}$, its average under any
	$\widetilde D_{\mathrm v,i}$ is at most $2m\delta$. If a nonnegative
	function of two line-point samples has average at most $\delta$ under
	$\widetilde D_{\mathrm{Line}}\times\widetilde D_{\mathrm{Line}}$,
	its average under any
	$\widetilde D_{\mathrm v_1,i}\times\widetilde D_{\mathrm v_2,j}$
	is at most $4m^2\delta$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	\leanok
	Every fiber of $\chi$ has size $q/m$. Partitioning the uniform seed by
	these fibers proves the mixture identities, retaining the seed when
	decoding. Each restriction has weight $1/(2m)$ in the full mixture;
	nonnegativity bounds its average by $2m$ times the full average. Apply
	this argument successively to the two coordinates for the product bound.
\end{proof}

Pushing the mixture identities forward, and composing nonnegative functions
with the seed-forgetting maps, gives the same identities and bounds for the
source laws. This accounts for the first two items of the following source
